Do not publish personalities for kexts whose start is skipped, except AMFI

Their classes never register, so every match on those personalities ends in
"Couldn't alloc class", which is what run-278's panel stops after.  AMFI is left
out of this list: its nub sits in the registry waiting for a driver while the
matching sweep keeps re-probing it, and that is the loop the panel is stuck in.
---
diff --git a/libkern/c++/OSKext.cpp b/libkern/c++/OSKext.cpp
--- a/libkern/c++/OSKext.cpp
+++ b/libkern/c++/OSKext.cpp
@@ -14801,6 +14801,23 @@ OSKext::sendPersonalitiesToCatalog(
 	bool      startMatching,
 	OSArray * personalityNames)
 {
+	/* nio: run-278 ends on the "Couldn't alloc class" lines and then nothing.
+	 * apfs and the security kexts have their start() skipped on purpose, so
+	 * their classes never register; publishing those personalities leaves
+	 * IOKit matching a class that cannot exist, so withhold them.
+	 * AMFI is a different case: its nub stays in the registry and waits for a
+	 * driver, its boot args are already permissive, and withholding its
+	 * personality only turns that nub into a permanent retry -- which is what
+	 * keeps the matching sweep from ever finishing. */
+	{
+		const char *nio_cid = getIdentifierCString();
+		if (nio_cid != NULL &&
+		    (strncmp(nio_cid, "com.apple.filesystems.apfs", 26) == 0 ||
+		    strncmp(nio_cid, "com.apple.security.", 19) == 0)) {
+			printf("nio: no personality for %s\n", nio_cid);
+			return kOSReturnSuccess;
+		}
+	}
 	OSReturn       result              = kOSReturnSuccess;
 	OSSharedPtr<OSArray> personalitiesToSend;
 	OSDictionary * kextPersonalities   = NULL;        // do not release
